        <div id="label" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. What Is in the Whey Isolate
          </h2>

          <p>
            The ingredient list on the flagship is short. Unflavored is just whey protein isolate. Flavored versions add natural flavors, a natural sweetener and, in some flavors, ingredients like cocoa or peanut. There is no sucralose, acesulfame potassium, aspartame, artificial color or filler. That is the core of the brand's pitch, and the label backs it up.
          </p>

          <p>
            Grass-fed is the other headline. Transparent Labs says its whey comes from grass-fed American dairy cattle raised without growth hormones, growth factors or anabolic steroids. The brand's page cites research suggesting grass-fed milk is higher in some fatty acids and bioactive compounds. Be realistic about what survives into an isolate, though. Isolation strips out almost all of the fat, so the fatty acid differences mostly do not reach the scoop. What you are mainly buying is a sourcing standard.
          </p>

          <p>
            The protein density is the more useful number. At 28 g of protein per 32 g scoop, about 87% of the powder by weight is protein, per the brand. That is high for a flavored product and tells you there is little room for fillers. Fat is about 1 g per scoop, and carbs range from 0 to 3 g depending on flavor. Transparent Labs describes it as virtually lactose-free, which is typical of a true isolate.
          </p>

          <p>
            On allergens, the brand says it tests for gluten and the eight major allergens, runs an allergen segregation program, and holds NSF certification at the facility level. That is a statement about the facility, not a product certification. The peanut-based flavors contain real peanuts. If you have a nut allergy, choose a flavor that has no peanut ingredients, and check the label, since the brand also processes coconut and peanut products in the same facility.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">WHAT IS AN ISOLATE?</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Isolate vs concentrate, in one line</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Whey isolate is filtered further than concentrate to remove most of the lactose and fat, which gives more protein per gram and fewer digestive complaints for lactose-sensitive people. It costs more to make. Our guide to <a href="/whey-vs-whey-isolate/">whey vs whey isolate</a> covers when the upgrade is worth paying for.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="testing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Certifications and Third-Party Testing
          </h2>

          <p>
            Transparent Labs leans heavily on testing, and it is one of the better-documented brands in the category. The whey isolate is <strong>Informed Choice</strong> certified, a program that screens for substances banned in sport, and <strong>Informed Protein</strong> certified, which checks that the protein content on the label matches what is in the tub. The mass gainer also carries Informed Choice certification, per the brand.
          </p>

          <p>
            The brand also says every batch is tested by a third-party lab for purity and potency, and it publishes those certificates of analysis on its website. That is more than most brands do. Reading a certificate takes a little practice, but the basic checks are simple: the protein figure should match the label, and heavy metal results should sit well below the limits listed on the report.
          </p>

          <p>
            Transparent Labs also cites a Labdoor score of 98 out of 100 for the whey isolate. Labdoor is an independent testing and ranking site. Its scores are useful context, but they are a snapshot of particular batches, not an ongoing certification, so give them less weight than Informed Choice. For tested athletes, the Informed Choice listing is what matters. Search the product and flavor on the certifier's site before you rely on it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Not every product in the range carries the same certification. The brand's pages for the casein, the Organic Vegan blend and the beef isolate do not mention Informed Choice in the text we reviewed. If certification is your reason for choosing Transparent Labs, buy the whey isolate or the mass gainer, and check the rest one by one. Our guide on <a href="/is-protein-powder-safe/">protein powder safety</a> explains what each type of testing covers.
          </p>
        </div>

