            7. Flavors, Taste and Mixing: What Buyers Report
          </h2>

          <p>
            We have not tasted these products, so this is a summary of buyer feedback. The whey isolate has thousands of reviews on the brand's site with a high average score, and the recurring praise is that it mixes easily and is less sweet than mainstream isolates. That fits the label. Without sucralose, flavors lean more natural and less candy-like, which some buyers love and others find flat.
          </p>

          <p>
            Flavor variety is a real strength. The comparison chart lists 22 flavors for the whey isolate, from chocolate and vanilla to dessert-style options like cinnamon French toast, chocolate chip cookie dough and blueberry pancakes, plus unflavored. Unflavored is the version to buy for cooking and smoothies. It is just whey isolate, and it disappears into oats, yogurt and pancakes. Our <a href="/best-unflavored-protein-powder/">unflavored protein guide</a> covers it alongside other plain options.
          </p>

          <p>
            The plant products get more mixed feedback. Reviews on the Organic Vegan page include praise for the clean ingredient list alongside complaints about strong stevia and a grainy texture, which is typical of rice and pea blends. Blending it with banana and nut butter, as several buyers describe doing, covers most of that. The casein mixes thicker than the whey, as all micellar casein does, which works in its favor for puddings.
          </p>

          <p>
            For the best result with the isolate, use 6-8 oz of cold water or milk in a shaker with a wire ball, as the brand suggests. Isolates froth if you blend them hard, so add them at the end of a smoothie and pulse briefly. With casein, start with less liquid than you think you need, then thin it out.
          </p>
