        <div id="who" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Who Should Buy Transparent Labs, and Who Should Skip It
          </h2>

          <p>
            <strong>Buy the Grass-Fed Whey Protein Isolate</strong> if you want a certified isolate with no artificial sweeteners, if you are lactose-sensitive and find concentrate blends bloat you, or if grass-fed sourcing matters to you. Buy the 4 lb tub once you have a flavor you like. It is the product that justifies the brand's reputation, and it is the one we would recommend first.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Buy the casein</strong> if you specifically want slow-digesting protein before bed or a thick pudding-style snack, and you like having the same grass-fed standard as the whey. <strong>Buy the beef isolate</strong> only if you avoid dairy and do not want plant protein. <strong>Buy the mass gainer</strong> only if you genuinely struggle to eat enough. At 750 calories a serving, it is not a casual add-on.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Skip Transparent Labs</strong> if you are on a tight budget. The same 25 g of protein is available for less from mainstream isolates and larger concentrate tubs. Skip the Organic Vegan blend if you are plant-only and want maximum protein per scoop, as the pea isolate or a dedicated plant brand will do better. Our <a href="/best-plant-based-protein-powder/">plant-based protein guide</a> lists the alternatives.
          </p>

          <p>
            As with any protein powder, if you have kidney disease, are pregnant, or take medication, talk to your doctor or a registered dietitian before adding concentrated protein or a creatine-containing gainer to your diet. This review covers label facts and value. It is not medical advice.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="use" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. How to Use It Well
          </h2>

          <p>
            One scoop of the isolate, at 28 g of protein, is about the dose research suggests most adults need per meal to maximize muscle protein synthesis. Larger or older lifters may benefit from a little more. Most people do not need two scoops at once. Spread your intake through the day instead, and use our <a href="/how-much-protein-do-i-need/">protein needs guide</a> to set a daily target before deciding how many shakes you need.
          </p>

          <p>
            Use the right product at the right time. The isolate digests quickly, so it suits mornings, post-workout and any time you want a light shake. The casein digests slowly and suits the evening or a long gap between meals. Combining both is not necessary for most people. Total daily protein matters far more than timing, as our guide on <a href="/when-to-take-protein-powder/">when to take protein powder</a> explains.
          </p>

          <p>
            Cook with the unflavored isolate rather than the flavored ones. It works in pancakes, oats, yogurt and baked goods, though isolates can dry baked goods out, so add a little extra liquid or fat. Keep the tub sealed and dry. Use the included scoop, because scoop sizes differ between Transparent Labs products, and borrowing a scoop from another brand will throw your numbers off.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are drug tested, keep a record of the batch number and check the Informed Choice listing whenever you rebuy. Certification applies to batches that were tested, and the brand's published certificates make that easy to check. That habit, not the brand name, is what protects a tested athlete.
          </p>
        </div>

